# Every flow node that belongs in a lane, with the layer whose lane it is. One row per (filing,
# layer, node), over both kinds of node a translation emits.
#
# A node count misses an orphan
#   An emission that put every call activity outside every lane would still match the count of call
#   activities against parts exactly. A BPMN reader of such a diagram sees nodes floating in a
#   process and cannot tell which layer any of them feeds, so which layer is composed from which is
#   gone from the rendering while the count is perfectly satisfied. That is a diagram returning the
#   right shape and a believable count, the failure `diagrams/roster.sqlc` exists to catch.
#
# Two kinds of node, labelled by the caller
#   They land in a lane for two different reasons:
#     a draw   an operation sits in the lane of the layer it draws from.
#     a part   a call sits in the lane of the layer it is composed into.
#   Neither branch knows which law it serves; the caller is what holds both.
#
# An operation that only induces has no lane
#   That is not an omission. A lane set of draws can only place what draws, so a notation cannot
#   place an operation that only commits something on another layer. Every operation on the corpus
#   draws, so the case is not exercised; the relation covers it anyway, because a future filing may
#   not draw.
-- entries/draws.sqlc and diagrams/foreign_calls.sqlc, each projected onto the lane it belongs to.
SELECT 'draw' AS node_kind, d.filing, d.layer
FROM (
    :compose(diagrams/lane_members.sqlc)
) d
UNION ALL
SELECT 'part', c.composition, c.composed_layer
FROM (
    :compose(diagrams/foreign_calls.sqlc)
) c
